            "diffs": [
              {
                "new_text": "# Context\n\nThe plot in `0_dataset_TESTING.ipynb` indexed `sample['ts'][:, 0, ...]` expecting HMI, but the flare_forecast config's channel order (`downstream_apps/flare_forecast/configs/config_script.yaml`) is:\n\n```\n[aia94, aia131, aia171, aia193, aia211, aia304, aia335, aia1600, hmi_m, hmi_bx, hmi_by, hmi_bz, hmi_v]\n```\n\nso index 0 is `aia94`. The user actually wants **AIA 131**, which is index **1**.\n\nSeparately, the user noticed a later cell in the same notebook plots frames correctly-looking by applying channel-aware display scaling (99th-percentile `vmax` + an SDO AIA colormap for AIA channels, or a fixed \u00b11000 G range with `hmimag`/`coolwarm` for HMI channels) instead of a bare `imshow`. Plain `imshow(frame)` on signum-log data with outlier pixels looks washed out/blank \u2014 that's the second reason the original two-panel plot didn't look right, independent of the channel-index bug.\n\n# Change\n\nIn `downstream_apps/flare_forecast/0_dataset_TESTING.ipynb`, rewrite the `sample_a`/`sample_b` comparison cell to:\n1. Index channel `1` (`aia131`) instead of `0`.\n2. Reuse the same scaling approach as the later reference cell: percentile-clip `vmax` (`np.percentile(frame[frame != 0], 99)`, `vmin=0`) and `cmap='sdoaia131'` for display, since this is an AIA channel (not HMI, so the `hmimag`/`coolwarm` branch doesn't apply).\n\nResulting cell:\n\n```python\nsample_a = None\nsample_b = None\nfor _ in range(50):  # keep drawing until you get one of each\n    s = train_dataset.__getitem__(0)\n    if s['was_flipped'] and sample_a is None:\n        sample_a = s\n    if not s['was_flipped'] and sample_b is None:\n        sample_b = s\n    if sample_a is not None and sample_b is not None:\n        break\n\nchannel = cfg.data.channels[1]  # aia131\n\nframe_a = train_dataset.inverse_transform_data(sample_a['ts'][:, 1, ...])[0]\nframe_b = train_dataset.inverse_transform_data(sample_b['ts'][:, 1, ...])[0]\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(8, 4))\nfor ax, frame, sample in [(axes[0], frame_a, sample_a), (axes[1], frame_b, sample_b)]:\n    lim = np.percentile(frame[frame != 0], 99)\n    ax.imshow(frame, cmap=f'sdo{channel}', vmin=0, vmax=lim)\n    ax.set_title(f\"was_flipped={sample['was_flipped']}\")\nplt.show()\n```\n\nAssumes `cfg` and `np` are already available earlier in the notebook (they are, per the reference cell). No other cells need to change.\n\n# Verification\n\nRe-run the cell and confirm both panels now show a plausible AIA 131 coronal image (bright active-region loop structures on a dark background), and that the `was_flipped=True` panel is a mirror image of the `was_flipped=False` one.",
