    {
      "body": "Yes \u2014 one `do_flip` draw per sample, applied uniformly to the whole stack:\n\n- `sample[\"ts\"]` (shape `(C, T, H, W)`) is flipped along `axis=-2`, which is height \u2014 so it's a vertical (up/down) flip, and it applies to **all channels and all input timesteps together** (not per-channel or per-frame independently).\n- `sample[\"forecast\"]` (the target frames) gets the **same** `do_flip` decision, so input and forecast stay spatially consistent with each other.\n- `sample[\"ground_truth\"]` (the scalar flare-intensity label) is untouched \u2014 it's a scalar, not spatial, so there's nothing to flip.\n\nSo: whole-stack, single coin-flip, vertical flip, shared between `ts` and `forecast`. One thing worth double-checking with the team: it's `axis=-2` (rows/height), not `axis=-1` \u2014 if you intended a left-right mirror (more common for solar image augmentation), you'd want `axis=-1` instead.",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789487172.0848837,
