      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "time": 1789487382.2622232,
      "body": "Good news \u2014 `num_mask_aia_channels` isn't set in this config (defaults to `0`) and `drop_hmi_probability: 0.0`, so there's no other source of randomness in `__getitem__`. That means you can force `flip_probability` to 0 and 1 and get an exact, byte-for-byte comparison instead of an eyeball check.\n\nReplace your cell with:\n\n```python\norig_p = train_dataset.flip_probability\n\ntrain_dataset.flip_probability = 0.0\nbaseline = train_dataset.__getitem__(0)\nassert baseline['was_flipped'] == False\n\ntrain_dataset.flip_probability = 1.0\nflipped = train_dataset.__getitem__(0)\nassert flipped['was_flipped'] == True\n\nnp.testing.assert_array_equal(flipped['ts'], np.flip(baseline['ts'], axis=-2))\nprint(\"confirmed: ts is a vertical flip (axis=-2) of the unflipped sample\")\n\ntrain_dataset.flip_probability = orig_p\n```\n\n- Lines 1 and 9 save/restore `flip_probability` so you don't leave the dataset in a forced state.\n- Lines 3\u20135 grab a guaranteed-unflipped baseline for index 0.\n- Lines 7\u20139 grab a guaranteed-flipped version of the *same* index.\n- Line 11 is the actual check: `np.testing.assert_array_equal` raises with a diff if the arrays don't match exactly, so you get a hard failure instead of trusting a printed flag or a visual comparison.",
      "id": "08952149-d3cf-46c9-9eef-77a145e87373",
      "type": "msg",
      "raw_time": false
    },
