      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Here's how the pieces relate, based on what's already in your notebook (cells 33\u201335) and `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py`:\n\n**Dataset vs. DataLoader**\n`flare_Dataset.__getitem__(idx)` (what you've been calling directly for the flip check) returns *one* sample. `DataLoader` wraps the dataset to do three things `__getitem__` alone doesn't: batch multiple samples together (stacking `ts`, `was_flipped`, etc. into tensors), shuffle the index order, and optionally parallelize loading across worker processes. Everything you tested so far (flip verification, channel plotting) bypassed the DataLoader entirely \u2014 you were calling the dataset directly, which is the right way to debug a single sample in isolation.\n\n**Your cell 35 vs. the production path**\n```python\ndata_loader = DataLoader(dataset=train_dataset, batch_size=2)\n```\nis a bare DataLoader \u2014 no `shuffle`, no `num_workers`, no seeding. Compare that to `build_helio_dataloaders()` in `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py`, which every actual training script uses instead of hand-rolling a `DataLoader`. It adds things that matter specifically for this dataset:\n- `worker_init_fn=_seed_worker` \u2014 reseeds `random`, `np.random`, *and* `torch` per worker. This matters because your flip draw (`np.random.rand() < flip_probability` in `flare_dataset.py`) and the channel masker both use ambient RNG state, not a generator you control. Without this, flip/mask behavior across workers isn't reproducible.\n- `multiprocessing_context=\"spawn\"` \u2014 required because the dataset holds an s3fs/boto3 handle that breaks under fork.\n- An explicit `torch.Generator` seeded from `cfg.seed` for the train shuffle, so epoch order depends only on the config, not on whatever global RNG state existed when you built the loader.\n- Only the train loader shuffles; val never does.\n\n**How to use this going forward**\n- Keep using `train_dataset.__getitem__(idx)` directly for the kind of single-sample checks you've been doing (flip verification, channel plotting) \u2014 it's cheaper and easier to reason about than batching.\n- Use your `data_loader` cell (35) sparingly, as the note already says, just to sanity-check *batching/collation* \u2014 e.g. pull one batch with `next(iter(data_loader))` and confirm `ts` comes out shaped `(B, C, T, H, W)` and `was_flipped` is a length-`B` tensor of independent draws (each item's flip is decided separately inside `__getitem__`).\n- When you move past this exploratory notebook into an actual training run, don't hand-roll `DataLoader(...)` \u2014 call `build_helio_dataloaders(cfg, flare_Dataset, ds_flare_index_path=..., ...)` instead, so you inherit the worker seeding and reproducible shuffle for free, the same way `3_finetune_template_1D.py` does."
    },
