      "id": "b4d8196b-7beb-494a-936f-cabde849d008",
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jpereira",
      "body": "what will that cell give me that'll help understand this? will it tell me how many will be flipped vs not flipped and then tell me an example plot of a flipped stack (2 inputs) and example of a not flipped stack? i think that'd convince me more. combining these three cells kinda: \n\nfor i in range(3):\n    sample = train_dataset.__getitem__(0)\n    print(f\"call {i}: was_flipped={sample['was_flipped']}\")\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport matplotlib.gridspec as gridspec\n\nanchor_time = pd.Timestamp(item['ds_index'])\ninput_offsets = cfg.data.time_delta_input_minutes\nframe_timestamps = [anchor_time + pd.Timedelta(minutes=off) for off in input_offsets]\nprint(\"Frame timestamps:\", frame_timestamps)\n\nn = 0\nchannel_order = cfg.data.channels\nchannel = channel_order[n]\nn_timesteps = item['ts'].shape[1]\n\nfig = plt.figure(figsize=np.array([4 * n_timesteps, 4]), dpi=100)\ngs = gridspec.GridSpec(1, n_timesteps, figure=fig, wspace=0.05, hspace=0)\n\nfor t in range(n_timesteps):\n    ts_frame = train_dataset.inverse_transform_data(item['ts'][:, t, ...])\n    plot_ts = ts_frame[n, ...]\n    print(f\"t={t} timestamp={frame_timestamps[t]} min={plot_ts.min():.4f} max={plot_ts.max():.4f} mean={plot_ts.mean():.4f}\")\n\n    ax = fig.add_subplot(gs[0, t])\n    if 'hmi' not in channel:\n        lim = np.percentile(plot_ts[plot_ts != 0], 99)\n        ax.imshow(plot_ts, cmap=f'sdo{channel}', vmin=0, vmax=lim)\n        font_color = 'w'\n    else:\n        font_color = 'k'\n        lim = 1000\n        if \"_v\" not in channel:\n            ax.imshow(plot_ts, cmap='hmimag', vmin=-lim, vmax=lim)\n        else:\n            ax.imshow(plot_ts, cmap='coolwarm', vmin=-lim, vmax=lim)\n\n    label = f\"{channel} @ {frame_timestamps[t]}\"\n    ax.text(0.01, 0.99, label, transform=ax.transAxes,\n            horizontalalignment='left', verticalalignment='top',\n            color=font_color, fontsize=5)\n    ax.set_xticks([])\n    ax.set_yticks([])\n\nplt.show()\n\nsample_a = None\nsample_b = None\nfor _ in range(50):  # keep drawing until you get one of each\n    s = train_dataset.__getitem__(0)\n    if s['was_flipped'] and sample_a is None:\n        sample_a = s\n    if not s['was_flipped'] and sample_b is None:\n        sample_b = s\n    if sample_a is not None and sample_b is not None:\n        break\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(8, 4))\nframe_a = train_dataset.inverse_transform_data(sample_a['ts'][:, 1, ...])[0]\nframe_b = train_dataset.inverse_transform_data(sample_b['ts'][:, 1, ...])[0]\naxes[0].imshow(frame_a, cmap='sdoaia131', vmin=0, vmax=np.percentile(frame_a[frame_a != 0], 99))\naxes[0].set_title(f\"was_flipped={sample_a['was_flipped']}\")\naxes[1].imshow(frame_b, cmap='sdoaia131', vmin=0, vmax=np.percentile(frame_b[frame_b != 0], 99))\naxes[1].set_title(f\"was_flipped={sample_b['was_flipped']}\")\nplt.show()"
    },
    {
      "body": "No \u2014 for this specific check, batching would make it harder, not easier, for two reasons:\n\n1. **You lose the \"same index, forced probability\" trick.** The check works because you force `flip_probability` to 0.0, grab index 0, then force it to 1.0 and grab index 0 again \u2014 same underlying image, controlled flip. A `DataLoader` batch pulls a *range* of indices (or shuffled ones), each with its own independent flip draw, and `default_collate` stacks them into a tensor. You'd have to disable shuffle and fish matching indices back out of two separate batches to reconstruct the same baseline/flipped pair you get for free right now.\n\n2. **`num_workers > 0` breaks live attribute mutation.** If you ever run this through a loader with `num_workers > 0` (like the real `build_helio_dataloaders()` setup), each worker process gets its own *pickled copy* of `train_dataset` when it spawns. Setting `train_dataset.flip_probability = 0.0` on the main process afterward doesn't reach already-spawned workers \u2014 so `orig_p`/`0.0`/`1.0` toggling would silently stop doing anything the moment workers are involved.\n\nKeep this check as direct `__getitem__` calls on the dataset (`num_workers=0`, no loader) \u2014 it's already the simplest, most reliable form. Save the DataLoader for checking batching/collation mechanics (shapes, dtypes), not for verifying the flip logic itself.The DataLoader still isn't the piece you need \u2014 `assert_array_equal` passing *is* mathematical proof, but it's silent, so it doesn't feel like proof. What you want is to *see* it. Add a plot to the same cell, reusing the imshow/scaling pattern from earlier:\n\n```python\norig_p = train_dataset.flip_probability\n\ntrain_dataset.flip_probability = 0.0\nbaseline = train_dataset.__getitem__(0)\nassert baseline['was_flipped'] == False\n\ntrain_dataset.flip_probability = 1.0\nflipped = train_dataset.__getitem__(0)\nassert flipped['was_flipped'] == True\n\nnp.testing.assert_array_equal(flipped['ts'], np.flip(baseline['ts'], axis=-2))\nprint(\"confirmed: ts is a vertical flip (axis=-2) of the unflipped sample\")\n\ntrain_dataset.flip_probability = orig_p\n\nchannel = cfg.data.channels[1]  # aia131\nbase_frame = train_dataset.inverse_transform_data(baseline['ts'][:, 1, ...])[0]\nflip_frame = train_dataset.inverse_transform_data(flipped['ts'][:, 1, ...])[0]\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(8, 4))\nfor ax, frame, label in [(axes[0], base_frame, \"baseline\"), (axes[1], flip_frame, \"flipped\")]:\n    lim = np.percentile(frame[frame != 0], 99)\n    ax.imshow(frame, cmap=f'sdo{channel}', vmin=0, vmax=lim)\n    ax.set_title(label)\nplt.show()\n```\n\nThat gives you both: the `assert` catches any silent regression later, and the two panels give you a picture you can eyeball to confirm the flip is vertical (top/bottom mirrored) and nothing else changed. A `DataLoader`/batch adds nothing here \u2014 it would only reintroduce the shuffle/worker problems from before.",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789488393.9881697,
      "raw_time": false,
      "mentions": [],
      "id": "7aa9966a-f6c6-438c-98dd-62bf8d62e631"
    },
    {
      "time": 1789488430.6436377,
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "6afea041-697e-4f91-aebc-ab607cabbdde",
      "body": "That combined cell would give you two kinds of proof instead of one: a **statistical check** (does the observed flip rate roughly match `flip_probability`, e.g. ~50/50 over many draws) and a **visual check** (do both input timesteps of a flipped stack move together, and does the flip look like a vertical mirror of the unflipped stack). Here's the merge:\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# 1. Empirical flip rate over many draws of the same index\nn_draws = 50\nflip_count = 0\nsample_flipped = None\nsample_not_flipped = None\nfor i in range(n_draws):\n    s = train_dataset.__getitem__(0)\n    if s['was_flipped']:\n        flip_count += 1\n        if sample_flipped is None:\n            sample_flipped = s\n    else:\n        if sample_not_flipped is None:\n            sample_not_flipped = s\n\nprint(f\"flip_probability={train_dataset.flip_probability}\")\nprint(f\"observed: {flip_count}/{n_draws} flipped ({flip_count/n_draws:.1%})\")\nassert sample_flipped is not None and sample_not_flipped is not None, \\\n    \"didn't see both outcomes in n_draws -- raise n_draws or check flip_probability\"\n\n# 2. Plot both input timesteps for one flipped example and one unflipped example\nchannel_idx = 1  # aia131\nchannel = cfg.data.channels[channel_idx]\nanchor_time = pd.Timestamp(sample_flipped['ds_index'])\nframe_timestamps = [anchor_time + pd.Timedelta(minutes=off) for off in cfg.data.time_delta_input_minutes]\nn_timesteps = sample_flipped['ts'].shape[1]\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(2, n_timesteps, figsize=(4 * n_timesteps, 8))\nfor row, (sample, row_label) in enumerate(\n    [(sample_not_flipped, \"was_flipped=False\"), (sample_flipped, \"was_flipped=True\")]\n):\n    for t in range(n_timesteps):\n        frame = train_dataset.inverse_transform_data(sample['ts'][:, t, ...])[channel_idx]\n        lim = np.percentile(frame[frame != 0], 99)\n        ax = axes[row, t]\n        ax.imshow(frame, cmap=f'sdo{channel}', vmin=0, vmax=lim)\n        ax.set_title(f\"{row_label}\\n{channel} t={t} ({frame_timestamps[t]})\", fontsize=8)\n        ax.set_xticks([]); ax.set_yticks([])\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n```\n\nWhat this tells you, concretely:\n- The printed ratio is your evidence the flip is actually probabilistic and roughly matches `train_dataset.flip_probability` (e.g. ~50% if it's 0.5), not always-on or always-off.\n- The 2\u00d7`n_timesteps` grid puts both timesteps of the flipped stack in the bottom row and both timesteps of the unflipped stack in the top row \u2014 same channel, same anchor time offsets. You can visually confirm two things at once: (a) the bottom row looks like a vertical mirror of the top row, and (b) within the bottom row, t=0 and t=1 are *both* mirrored the same way \u2014 proving the flip is applied consistently across the whole stack, not per-frame independently (which is what your earlier `flare_dataset.py` reading already told us, but now you can see it).\n\nNote this uses `sample_flipped['ds_index']` for timestamps, but since all draws are `__getitem__(0)`, every sample shares the same anchor \u2014 only the flip differs.",
