      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "Yes \u2014 this is exactly a config change, no code edit needed. The catalog CSV already has a `peak_time` column alongside `start_time`:\n\n```\nstart_time,peak_time,end_time,GOES_class,AR,CMX,CMX_VALUE,intensity\n```\n\nAnd `flare_dataset.py:70` just does `self.ds_index[ds_time_column]` \u2014 it looks up whatever column name you pass via the config's `ds_time_column`, with no hardcoding of `start_time`. So in `downstream_apps/flare_forecast/configs/config_script.yaml`, change:\n\n```yaml\nds_time_column: start_time\n```\nto\n```yaml\nds_time_column: peak_time\n```\n\nOne thing worth thinking about before you flip this, though: `ds_match_direction: forward` is documented (`flare_dataset.py:30-31`) as \"causal prediction (predict flares from prior solar state)\" \u2014 i.e. it matches each Surya timestamp to the *next* flare event timestamp, so the model only ever sees solar state *before* the event it's predicting. That reasoning holds for `start_time` (predicting a flare before it starts). If you switch to `peak_time`, `forward` matching would pick the Surya state before the *peak*, which could land *after* the flare has already started \u2014 meaning the model could be trained on input frames that already show the flare in progress, not just its precursor state. Whether that's what you want depends on whether your goal is still \"forecast a flare before onset\" (in which case keep matching against `start_time` for causality, but maybe carry `peak_time` through separately for analysis) or \"predict peak intensity given knowledge the flare has started\" (a different, less strict task). Worth deciding intentionally rather than as a side effect of the config change.",
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "4336cd29-3393-46c5-80cb-3aa5796695ae",
      "time": 1789496085.6272795,
      "raw_time": false
    }
  ],
